\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20 {\cs16\b\f1\fs24\super #{\footnote \pard\plain \s15\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20 {\cs16\super #}{contents}}}{\b\f1\fs24   }{\b\f1\fs24\cf2 Contents
\par }{\f1 
\par }\pard \widctlpar\brdrt\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp20 \adjustright {\f1\uldb 
\par }\pard \widctlpar\adjustright {\f1 This file should be accessed only via the Hungarian HexaHedron program.\page }{\cs16\f1\super #{\footnote \pard\plain \s15\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20 {\cs16\super #}{ hint0}}}{\f1  }{\b\f1\fs72\cf2 0}{\b\f1\fs24\cf2 
 Hint
\par }{\f1\uldb 
\par }\pard \widctlpar\brdrt\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp20 \adjustright {\f1 
\par }\pard \widctlpar\adjustright {\f1 No hints.  You're on your own!
\par 
\par Actually, there is one philosophical hint you probably already know about which is that the satisfaction of success is proporti
onal to the difficulty of the task.  If you figure out the Cube without any hints, then you will have achieved the greatest success.  There won't be any graphics or sound effects from this program though, but you'll know when you have the Cube mastered.

\par 
\par I
f you need a hint, or you're pretty sure you've got the basic idea of how you'll have to proceed to solve it yourself, then go ahead and look at the next hint.   This series of hints was designed to be long enough that you aren't likely to find out too mu
ch too soon.
\par 
\par To see the next hint, select "Hint" under "Help" in the main window.  Another sub-menu will appear from which you can select any hint, but you will be warned before you are shown a different hint.  You can decrease the hint number too, but onc
e a secret is out you aren't likely to forget it.  You can also select "Hint" under "Help" in a file window.  This will immediately show you the current hint.
\par 
\par This program saves the hint number in the file HHH.INI in the directory with this program so it 
will be kept up to date.  But if someone else is working on solving the Cube on your computer, make sure you check the hint number before you read your hint or you might find out more than you want to know.
\par 
\par Good luck and Congratulations on solving the Cube, at whatever level you succeed!\page }{\cs16\f1\super #{\footnote \pard\plain \s15\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20 {\cs16\super #}{ hint1}}}{\f1  }{\b\f1\fs72\cf2 1}{\b\f1\fs24\cf2  Hint
\par }{\f1 
\par }\pard \widctlpar\brdrt\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp20 \adjustright {\f1 
\par }\pard \widctlpar\adjustright {\f1 The first hint is to try solving a single layer of the Cube without worrying about the rest.
\par 
\par If this hint helps you and you learn to solve one layer, think about what you've learned before you go on to the next
 hint.  You might have learned something else about the Cube along the way and you don't want to spoil that discovery by reading the next hint too soon.
\par 
\par If this hint seems obvious, think again.  You might be able to learn more from it than you expect.\page }{\cs16\f1\super #{\footnote \pard\plain \s15\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20 {\cs16\super #}{ hint2}}}{\f1  }{\b\f1\fs72\cf2 2}{\b\f1\fs24\cf2  Hint

\par }{\f1 
\par }\pard \widctlpar\brdrt\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp20 \adjustright {\f1\uldb 
\par }\pard \widctlpar\adjustright {\f1 The second hint is to continue solving in "plateau states."
\par 
\par While solving only one layer might not look too impressive to your friends, you have accomplished a great deal.  Besides the center, you have gotten four edge and four corner cubies in th
eir proper locations and orientations, but that's not the half of it.  The more important thing is that they are no longer mixed up with the rest of the Cube!  There are only eight edges and four corners left to correct and they can only be mixed up among
 themselves, they are no longer mixed up with the four edges and corners you have already fixed.
\par 
\par In fact, the number of ways to mix the locations of the 12 edges was 479,001,600.  But there are only 8 mixed up now, so the number is down to 40,320.  This is
 an improvement of a factor of 11,880.  The corners are even closer.  The number of ways to mix the locations of the original eight corners was 40,320, but there are only 4 now, so that number is only 24 so that is an improvement of a factor of 1,680.

\par 
\par Now try expanding the idea of solving one face into solving the Cube through a set of plateau states.  Try solving the Cube one stage at a time.  Each time you get more of the Cube in order, the scrambled part becomes more organized in itself.\page }{
\cs16\f1\super #{\footnote \pard\plain \s15\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20 {\cs16\super #}{ hint3}}}{\f1  }{\b\f1\fs72\cf2 3}{\b\f1\fs24\cf2  Hint
\par }{\f1 
\par }\pard \widctlpar\brdrt\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp20 \adjustright {\f1\uldb 
\par }\pard \widctlpar\adjustright {\f1 The third hint is to play the conservative game.
\par 
\par As you develop an idea of what should be done in the plateau states, don't expect to accomplish too much at each stage.  Your problem now is that you have the Cube partially solved and you can't mess that up.  
So you will have to play a more "conservative" game.
\par 
\par Of course with this program, unlike real life, you have an infinite supply of Cubes and a record of what you've done.  Save your Cube to a file or erase moves from the Generator box in case you make a mistake.\page }{\cs16\f1\super #{\footnote 
\pard\plain \s15\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20 {\cs16\super #}{ hint4}}}{\f1  }{\b\f1\fs72\cf2 4}{\b\f1\fs24\cf2  Hint
\par }{\f1 
\par }\pard \widctlpar\brdrt\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp20 \adjustright {\f1\uldb 
\par }\pard \widctlpar\adjustright {\f1 The fourth hint is idea of "operators" or "tools."
\par 
\par For the Cube, you will benefit from a collection of these.  You may have discovered some useful tools already.  Save your scrambled Cube to a file and work with a new Cube.  Try one of 
the tricks you know about on the new Cube and you will see how the trick rearranges part of the Cube and leaves the rest undisturbed.  This is a tool for your toolbox.
\par 
\par Save these tools in a file.  In the old days, Cube solvers had to record these on paper
 and reproduce them on a real Cube perfectly each time.  But with this program, you can store your tools, or operators, in a file and let the computer apply them to your scrambled Cube.  When you double-click on a file item and get the File-Cube Operation
 
dialog box, the last two choices are "Apply File Item Operator to Main Cube" and "Apply Inverse File Item Operator to Main Cube."  If you need to use an operator you've stored in a file to transform a scrambled Cube in the main window, use these commands 
to apply your operator or its opposite to a scrambled Cube.
\par 
\par Of course, you will need more than one tool because there is no single trick that solves all the kinds of problems that occur in solving the Cube.
\par 
\par When describing an operator it is useful to have
 a notation to say what cubies get transformed.  A standard notation has been established for referring to a cubie.  Lower case letters are used to list the faces in which a cubie has a facelet.  Center cubies have one letter, edges two and corners three.
 
 The move R causes the transformations (br, dr, fr, ur) and (urf, bru, drb, frd)  which means the br edge cubie moves to dr, dr moves to fr, fr to ur and ur back to br.  A similar thing happens to the corners.  The order of letters referencing a corner cu
bie should be clockwise looking at it corner-on.  
\par 
\par There is no restriction on the starting point and an edge reference could be either "br" or "rb".  But there is meaning from one cubie reference to the next.  In listing "br, dr" it means the b facelet mov
es to the d facelet and the r to the r.  Listing "br, dr" instead would imply a flip.  In (br, dr, fr, ur), ur moves to br.  Sometimes a transformation can't be written correctly because the last cubie is flipped on its way to the first cubie's position, 
s
o a plus sign + is written after the right parenthesis.  Corners work similarly except if there is a plus sign at the end it implies a 120 degree twist from the last cubie to the first cubie's position and a minus sign implies 240 degrees.  The move seque
nce RU can be written (ur, br, dr, fr, uf, ul, ub) (urf)+ (ubr, bdr, dfr, luf, bul)-.  This program does not use this notation, but it is presented in case it is useful to you.\page }{\cs16\f1\super #{\footnote \pard\plain \s15\widctlpar\adjustright 
\fs20 {\cs16\super #}{ hint5}}}{\f1  }{\b\f1\fs72\cf2 5}{\b\f1\fs24\cf2  Hint
\par }{\f1 
\par }\pard \widctlpar\brdrt\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp20 \adjustright {\f1\uldb 
\par }\pard \widctlpar\adjustright {\f1 The fifth hint is to refine the "operators" or "tools."
\par 
\par If you were trying to take off a bolt and you didn't have many tools, you might use a pair of pliers.  But if the bolt was tight, you would be much better off with an adjustable wrench.  And it would better still if you had a box wrench.
\par 
\par  As you experiment more and g
et better at it, you'll be able to narrow the focus of your operators so you can make specific things happen without changing the rest of the Cube.  You will have better tools.  The better the tools you develop, the easier it will be for you to apply them
 to specific problems.\page }{\cs16\f1\super #{\footnote \pard\plain \s15\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20 {\cs16\super #}{ hint6}}}{\f1  }{\b\f1\fs72\cf2 6}{\b\f1\fs24\cf2  Hint
\par }{\f1 
\par }\pard \widctlpar\brdrt\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp20 \adjustright {\f1\uldb 
\par }\pard \widctlpar\adjustright {\f1 The sixth hint is the use of conjugates.
\par 
\par If you haven't discovered this hint but have discovered a collection of refined operators, you almost certainly have been using this idea already.
\par 
\par If you have a refined operator th
at transforms a small number of cubies and need an operator that does the same thing except on different cubies it is easy to create a conjugate of the original operator.  Using any sequence of moves, maneuver the cubies into the locations so they are whe
r
e the original operator expects them and carry out this operator.  Then do the inverse of the first sequence that got them there.  Doing a sequence of moves and later undoing it with your operator sandwiched in between works like doing your operator on a 
new set of cubies.
\par 
\par Suppose you wanted to swap two pairs of edge cubies and didn't care about scrambling the corners.  Obviously F\'b2 exchanges opposite edges on the Front face.  Using the notation described in the hint 4, the exchange can be written (fu, fd)
 (fr, fl).  Suppose instead of the (fu, fd) pair in the Front face, you wanted to exchange the (ru, rd) pair in the Right face.  You could get them in position by doing Us (Upper slice).  Then you could do F\'b2 and reposition them with Us'.  So UsF\'b2
Us' swaps (ru, rd) (fr, fl), ignoring the corners.
\par 
\par In this program you can write a conjugate of two operators using a star *, so this example is written Us * F\'b2 and read "the conjugate of F\'b2 by Us."  By writing it this way, it is clear what is going on and it is l
ess likely that you will make a typographical error writing the inverse of the first operation at the end, if the operation is long and complicated.
\par 
\par Of course, F\'b2 is a lousy operator for swapping edges because it scrambles the corners and you'll want to d
iscover a better one.  But the conjugate trick applied to good operators will vastly expand your repertoire because it is an easy way to take an operator that works on only one part of the Cube and make it work anywhere.\page }{\cs16\f1\super #{\footnote 
\pard\plain \s15\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20 {\cs16\super #}{ hint7}}}{\f1  }{\b\f1\fs72\cf2 7}{\b\f1\fs24\cf2  Hint
\par }{\f1 
\par }\pard \widctlpar\brdrt\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp20 \adjustright {\f1\uldb 
\par }\pard \widctlpar\adjustright {\f1 The seventh hint is a li
st of the operator types you will need.  Using conjugates, you can do anything.  This hint is only to describe what the operators do and it is up to you to discover how to do them.  Subsequent hints will give examples of how to do each.
\par 
\par }{\f1\cf6 Edge Swapper: Dual 2-cycle }{\i\f1\cf6 or}{\f1\cf6  Single 3-cycle}{\f1 
\par \tab You need an operator to exchange the locations of edge cubies, the fewer the better, without effecting the rest of the Cube.  It is not possible to swap only one pair of edges unless you take the Cube apart.  You can swap two in
dependent pairs, a dual edge 2-cycle, or swap two pairs with one cubie in common, an edge 3-cycle.  Examples of these operators is the 8th hint.
\par 
\par }{\f1\cf6 Corner Swapper: Dual 2-cycle }{\i\f1\cf6 or}{\f1\cf6  Single 3-cycle}{\f1 
\par \tab You need an operator to exchange the locations of corner cubie
s, the fewer the better, without effecting the rest of the Cube.  It is not possible to swap only one pair of corners unless you take the Cube apart.  You can swap two independent pairs, a dual corner 2-cycle, or swap two pairs with one cubie in common, a
 corner 3-cycle.  Examples of these operators is the 9th hint.
\par 
\par }{\f1\cf6 Two Edge Flipper}{\f1 
\par \tab You need an operator to flip edge cubies in place, the fewer the better, without effecting the rest of the Cube.  It is not possible to flip only one edge unless you take the 
Cube apart.  You can do a two edge flipper.  An example of this operator is the 10th hint.
\par 
\par }{\f1\cf6 Corner Twister: Two (opposite directions) }{\i\f1\cf6 or}{\f1\cf6  Three (same direction)
\par }{\f1 \tab You need an operator to twist corner cubies in place, the fewer the better, without effecting t
he rest of the Cube.  It is not possible to twist only one corner unless you take the Cube apart.  You can twist two corners in opposite directions (one 120 degrees, the other 240 degrees) or you can do this on two overlapping pairs with one cubie in comm
on so as to twist three corners in the same direction (all 120 degrees or all 240 degrees).  Examples of these operators is the 11th hint.
\par \page }{\cs16\f1\super #{\footnote \pard\plain \s15\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20 {\cs16\super #}{ hint8}}}{\f1  }{\b\f1\fs72\cf2 8}{\b\f1\fs24\cf2  Hint
\par }{\f1 
\par }\pard \widctlpar\brdrt\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp20 \adjustright {\f1\uldb 
\par }\pard \widctlpar\adjustright {\f1 The eighth hint is how to do a dual edge 2-cycle and a single edge 3-cycle.
\par 
\par The first operator swaps the opposite pairs of edge cubies in the Up face.  The second operator cycles three edge cubies clockwise in the Up face, the three that also lie in the Right, Front and Left faces.
\par 
\par Dual edge 2-cycle (uf,ub) (ur,ul): R\'b2F\'b2U\'b2 * (F\'b2R\'b2)3\line Edge 3-cycle (ur,uf,ul): F\'b2ULsF\'b2Ls'UF\'b2\line 
\par You can copy these by hand or you can Cut and Paste them from this document through the Clipboard.  Drag the mouse across them so they become highlighted and press Ctrl+C or Ctrl+Insert or select "Copy" under "Edit" on the menu.  Now they're
 in the Clipboard.  Now go into a Cube File and Paste them by pressing Ctrl+V or Shift+Insert or select "Paste" under "Edit" on the menu.
\par 
\par Hint 9 is a dual corner 2-cycle and a single corner 3-cycle.
\par Hint 10 is a two edge flipper.
\par Hint 11 is a two corner twist (opposite directions) and a three corner twist (same direction).\page }{\cs16\f1\super #{\footnote \pard\plain \s15\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20 {\cs16\super #}{ hint9}}}{\f1  }{\b\f1\fs72\cf2 9}{\b\f1\fs24\cf2  Hint
\par }{\f1 
\par }\pard \widctlpar\brdrt\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp20 \adjustright {\f1\uldb 
\par }\pard \widctlpar\adjustright {\f1 The ninth hint is how to do a dual corner 2-cycle and a single corner 3-cycle.
\par 
\par The first operator swaps the opposite pairs of corner cubies in the Up face.  The second operator cycles three corner cubies clockwise in the Up face, the three that also lie in the Right and Front faces.
\par 
\par Dual corner 2-cycle (urf, ulb) (ubr, ufl): U\'b2(R\'b2F\'b2U\'b2 * (F\'b2R\'b2)3)\line Corner 3-cycle (ubr,urf,ufl): R\'b2F\'b2R'B'RF\'b2R'BR'\line 
\par You can copy these by hand or you
 can Cut and Paste them from this document through the Clipboard.  Drag the mouse across them so they become highlighted and press Ctrl+C or Ctrl+Insert or select "Copy" under "Edit" on the menu.  Now they're in the Clipboard.  Now go into a Cube File and
 Paste them by pressing Ctrl+V or Shift+Insert or select "Paste" under "Edit" on the menu.
\par 
\par Hint 8 is a dual edge 2-cycle and a single edge 3-cycle.
\par Hint 10 is a two edge flipper.
\par Hint 11 is a two corner twist (opposite directions) and a three corner twist (same direction).\page }{\cs16\f1\super #{\footnote \pard\plain \s15\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20 {\cs16\super #}{ hint10}}}{\f1  }{\b\f1\fs72\cf2 10}{\b\f1\fs24\cf2  Hint
\par }{\f1 
\par }\pard \widctlpar\brdrt\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp20 \adjustright {\f1\uldb 
\par }\pard \widctlpar\adjustright {\f1 The tenth hint is how to do a two edge flipper.
\par 
\par These operators flip the Up-Front edge cubie in place and one other.  The first also flips the Up-Left cubie, the second the Up-Back cubie and the third the Up-Right.  All three are given because they are so similar in form.
\par 
\par Two edge flipper (uf)+ (ul)+: (FUsL\'b2Us\'b2R)+U'\line Two edge flipper (uf)+ (ub)+: (FUsL\'b2Us\'b2R)+U\'b2\line Two edge flipper (uf)+ (ur)+: (FUsL\'b2Us\'b2R)+U\line 
\par You can copy these by hand or you can Cut and Paste them from this docum
ent through the Clipboard.  Drag the mouse across them so they become highlighted and press Ctrl+C or Ctrl+Insert or select "Copy" under "Edit" on the menu.  Now they're in the Clipboard.  Now go into a Cube File and Paste them by pressing Ctrl+V or Shift
+Insert or select "Paste" under "Edit" on the menu.
\par 
\par Hint 8 is a dual edge 2-cycle and a single edge 3-cycle.
\par Hint 9 is a dual corner 2-cycle and a single corner 3-cycle.
\par Hint 11 is a two corner twist (opposite directions) and a three corner twist (same direction).\page }{\cs16\f1\super #{\footnote \pard\plain \s15\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20 {\cs16\super #}{ hint11}}}{\f1  }{\b\f1\fs72\cf2 11}{\b\f1\fs24\cf2  Hint
\par }{\f1 
\par }\pard \widctlpar\brdrt\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp20 \adjustright {\f1\uldb 
\par }\pard \widctlpar\adjustright {\f1 The eleventh hint is how to do a two corner twist (opposite directions) and a three corner twist (same direction).
\par 
\par The first three operations twist the Up-Right-Front cubie in place 120 degrees clockwise and one other 120 degrees coun
terclockwise.  The first also twists the Up-Front-Left cubie, the second the Up-Left-Back and the third the Up-Back-Right.  The last operator twists three corner cubies 120 degrees clockwise in the Up face, the three that also lie in the Right and Front f
aces.  The inverse of this last operator would do a counterclockwise twist on the same cubies.
\par 
\par Two corner twist (urf)+ (ufl)-: (R' * D)(F * D)+U'\line Two corner twist (urf)+ (ulb)-: (R' * D)(F * D)+U\'b2\line Two corner twist (urf)+ (ubr)-: (R' * D)(F * D)+U\line Three corner twist (ubr)+ (urf)+ (ufl)+: RU\'b2R'F\'b2L\'b2BL'(FL')\'b2B'L\line 

\par You can copy these by hand or you can Cut and Paste them from this document through the Clipboard.  Drag the mouse across them so they become highlighted and press Ctrl+C or Ctrl+Insert or select "Copy
" under "Edit" on the menu.  Now they're in the Clipboard.  Now go into a Cube File and Paste them by pressing Ctrl+V or Shift+Insert or select "Paste" under "Edit" on the menu.
\par 
\par Hint 8 is a dual edge 2-cycle and a single edge 3-cycle.
\par Hint 9 is a dual corner 2-cycle and a single corner 3-cycle.
\par Hint 10 is a two edge flipper.\page }{\cs16\f1\super #{\footnote \pard\plain \s15\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20 {\cs16\super #}{ hint12}}}{\f1  }{\b\f1\fs72\cf2 12}{\b\f1\fs24\cf2  Hint
\par }{\f1 
\par }\pard \widctlpar\brdrt\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp20 \adjustright {\f1\uldb 
\par }\pard \widctlpar\adjustright {\f1 The twelfth and final hint is a veritable cheat sheet.  If you can't solve the Cube with these instructions, then you probably aren't going to enjoy this program much anyway.
\par 
\par The steps you need to take are outlined, then explained, and finally the operators you will need are listed.  (Most of the operators are the same as from the preceding hints.)
\par 
\par }{\f1\cf1 1. Solve the Down layer.
\par }\pard \fi720\widctlpar\adjustright {\f1\cf1 1A. Solve the edge cubies in the Down layer.
\par 1B. Solve the corner cubies in the Down layer.
\par }\pard \widctlpar\adjustright {\f1\cf1 2. Solve the middle layer (four edge cubies).
\par 3. Solve the Up layer.
\par }\pard \fi720\widctlpar\adjustright {\f1\cf1 3A. Position the edge cubies (ignoring flip).
\par 3B. Position the corner cubies (ignoring twist).
\par 3C. Unflip the edge cubies.
\par 3D. Untwist the corner cubies.}{\f1\cf2 
\par }\pard \widctlpar\adjustright {\f1 
\par At any step it is often useful to rotate the whole Cube about the Up layer so the cubie(s) you are interested in working on are in front where you can see them.  Whenever you do this, though, you will have to keep an eye on the center facelets fo
r reference.  You are really only concerned that the facelets match the centers, but when you rotate the whole Cube you have to remember that these reference colors have changed.  When you use operators out of a Cube File, these are referenced to the stan
dard color pattern and your color pattern will have shifted so you must transpose the colors in your head.
\par 
\par While working on the Down and middle layers you will be working on four symmetrically similar cubies at a time.  Solve these in four steps rotating t
he whole Cube about the Up face 90 degrees at a time.  It is sometimes better, though, to skip a cubie of the four and come back to it after you've solved the others, especially if you find the cubie you want in the position of one of the other four.  If 
you solve that cubie first instead, obviously the other cubie will be moved somewhere else where it will probably be easier to get at.
\par 
\par 
\par }{\b\f1\cf2 1. Solve the Down layer.}{\f1\cf2 
\par }{\f1 
\par You must get the whole layer, not just the face solved.  It's no use getting the bottom layer 
cubies to match unless the facelets on the sides of the cubies also match the center facelet in that face.  It is easier to solve the Down (green) layer if you turn the whole Cube upside-down while you are working. While you are solving the Down layer, do
n't worry about the rest of the Cube.
\par 
\par While working on the green layer, it is never a good idea to turn the face itself.  Use moves of the other faces.
\par  
\par Because so much of the Cube is disordered at this stage, the cubie that you are trying to get correcte
d could be in virtually any position and orientation and there is very little ordered in the Cube that you need worry about rescrambling, so no operators are provided.  You'll have to choose your moves yourself.
\par 
\par }{\b\f1\cf2 1A. Solve the edge cubies in the Down layer.
\par }{\f1 
\par It is easier to solve the edges first and worry about the corners later because the edges lie in only one face besides the green face.  You should never rotate the green face at this stage and it is only rarely necessary to rotate the other face that a 
solved edge cubie is in.  If you do need to rotate the other face, you can rotate it 90 degrees to get the cubie you want to work on in the blue face, rotate the blue face to get the cubie out of the way and rotate the first face back to where it was.  Yo
u can do most of the shuttling of the cubie in the blue face.
\par 
\par }{\b\f1\cf2 1B. Solve the corner cubies in the Down layer.}{\f1 
\par 
\par To solve a corner cubie in the green layer, you must first get it in the blue layer but its green facelet can not be in the blue face.  If you wan
t to work on a cubie that meets these conditions, you can see that there are two non-green faces where the corner cubie should go.  Select one of these faces.  Get the corner cubie out of that face by turning the blue face if necessary.  Turn the selected
 
face 90 degrees so that the place the corner cubie should go is now in the blue face and then turn the blue face so the corner cubie is in its proper position.  The corner cubie now lies next to and appears properly oriented with the adjacent edge cubie i
n
 that face, unless you've chosen the wrong one of the two non-green faces where the corner cubie should go.  If you have chosen incorrect, the error will be obvious.  Undo what you've done and try again with the other one of the two faces.  In either case
, the final step is to rotate the face so both the edge and the newly corrected corner are put into the green face.
\par 
\par The preceding paragraph presumed the corner cubie you were interested in correcting was in the blue layer and the cubie's green facelet was 
not in the blue face.  If this is not so, other steps must first be taken to correct those problems and then the procedure above can be followed.  If the cubie is not in the blue layer it is in the green layer.  Select one of the two non-green layers the 
cubie is in and rotate it 90 degrees to get it in the blue layer, then rotate the blue face to get the cubie out of the way and rotate the selected face back where it was.
\par 
\par If the cubie's green facelet is in the blue face, rotate the blue face so the cubie 
is opposite in the blue face where it belongs in the green face.  Select one of the two non-green faces where the cubie should go and rotate it 90 degrees, rotate the blue face to get the cubie out of the way and rotate the selected face back where it was
.
\par 
\par }{\b\f1\cf2 2. Solve the middle layer (four edge cubies).}{\f1 
\par 
\par If you've turned the Cube upside down to solve the green layer, turn it back.  Don't worry about scrambling the Up face during this step.
\par 
\par Do this step on each of the four edge cubies by rotating the whole Cube about the Up face so that the cubie you want to solve will go to the Front-Right edge.
\par 
\par Find the edge cubie you want to correct.  It is either in the Up face or in the middle, possibly it is in the proper location but flipped in place.  If it is in the
 Up face, rotate the Up face so the cubie is also in either the Front or the Right face.  In one of these two positions, the edge cubie's Front or Right facelet will match the center of that face.  If it matches the Front, put it there and use the operato
r "Middle Front" listed below.  If it matches the Right, put it in the Right face and do the "Middle Right" operator listed below.
\par 
\par If the edge cubie you want to correct is along the middle, you must first get it out by doing either of these two operators, 
but you might want to save those problems for last in this step because solving another cubie will reposition this one.  (Of course, if the two cubies are in each other's place, or the cubie is just flipped in place this strategy is worthless.)
\par 
\par }{\b\f1\cf2 3. Solve the Up layer.
\par }{\f1 
\par By now the Cube is reasonably well ordered and the work becomes very detailed.  Since all the cubies left to solve are in the Up face,  there is no need to refer to the Up face in describing the cubie, so in the following instructions the Up-
Back edge cubie, for example, is referred to as the Back edge cubie.
\par 
\par }{\b\f1\cf2 3A. Position the edge cubies (ignoring flip).}{\f1 
\par 
\par In this step, an edge flipped in place is considered to be in its proper position.
\par 
\par Rotate the Up face so that the Back edge cubie is in it
s proper position.  Count how many edge cubies are out of position.  If the number is zero you're done.  If it is three and they need to be rotated clockwise among themselves, do the "Edge 3-cycle" operator listed below.  If they must go counterclockwise 
do the inverse of that operator.
\par 
\par If the number is two and the Right cubie is in the proper position, do a U move, the "Edge 3-cycle" operator and a U\'b2 move.  If the number is two and the Left cubie is in the proper position, do a U\'b2 move, the operator and a U move.
\par 
\par The only other possibility is that the number is two and the Front edge cubie is in the proper position.  Do a U move, the "Edge 3-cycle" operator, a U\'b2 and the inverse of the operator.
\par 
\par }{\b\f1\cf2 3B. Position the corner cubies (ignoring twist).}{\f1 
\par 
\par In this step, a corner twisted in place is considered to be in its proper position.
\par 
\par Count how many corners are out of position.  If it's zero, you're done. If it's three, rotate the whole Cube about the Up face so that the Back-Left cubie is the only cubie in pos
ition.  If the other three need to be rotated clockwise among themselves, do the "Corner 3-cycle" operator listed below.  Otherwise they must go counterclockwise, so do the inverse of that operator.
\par 
\par If all four corners are out of position and opposite corners need to be swapped, do the "Dual corner 2-cycle" operator listed below.  Otherwise, do the "Corner 3-cycle" and repeat this step.
\par 
\par }{\b\f1\cf2 3C. Unflip the edge cubies.}{\f1 
\par 
\par If all four edges are flipped in place, do any of the "Edge flipper" operators listed below
 and you will have two left.  To unflip two, turn the whole Cube about the Up face so one is in the Front face and do one of the three operators: the first if the other flipped edge is in the Left face, the second if it's in the Back and the third if it's
 in the Right.
\par 
\par }{\b\f1\cf2 3D. Untwist the corner cubies.}{\f1 
\par 
\par Count the number of corners twisted in place.  If the number is three, turn the whole Cube so the Back-Left cubie is the only one not twisted.  The other three all need to be twisted in the same direction.  If
 (looking corner on) they need to go 120 degrees clockwise, do the "Three corner twist" operator listed below.  If they need to go 120 degrees counterclockwise, do the inverse of that operator.  If the number is four, do the operator and repeat this step.

\par 
\par If the number is two, rotate the whole Cube so the cubie that needs to go clockwise is the Front-Right cubie.  Do one of the three "Two corner twist" operators listed below: the first if the other twisted corner is the Front-Left, the second if it's the L
eft-Back and the third if it's the Back-Right.
\par 
\par 
\par }{\b\f1\cf2 Operators
\par }{\f1 
\par Middle Front:U\'b2F'U\'b2L'U\'b2LU\'b2F\line Middle Right:U\'b2RU\'b2BU\'b2B'U\'b2R'\line Dual edge 2-cycle (uf,ub) (ur,ul): R\'b2F\'b2U\'b2 * (F\'b2R\'b2)3\line Edge 3-cycle (ur,uf,ul): F\'b2ULsF\'b2Ls'UF\'b2\line 
Dual corner 2-cycle (urf, ulb) (ubr, ufl): U\'b2(R\'b2F\'b2U\'b2 * (F\'b2R\'b2)3)\line Corner 3-cycle (ubr,urf,ufl): R\'b2F\'b2R'B'RF\'b2R'BR'\line Two edge flipper (uf)+ (ul)+: (FUsL\'b2Us\'b2R)+U'\line Two edge flipper (uf)+ (ub)+: (FUsL\'b2Us\'b2R)+U
\'b2\line Two edge flipper (uf)+ (ur)+: (FUsL\'b2Us\'b2R)+U\line Two corner twist (urf)+ (ufl)-: (R' * D)(F * D)+U'\line Two corner twist (urf)+ (ulb)-: (R' * D)(F * D)+U\'b2\line Two corner twist (urf)+ (ubr)-: (R' * D)(F * D)+U\line 
Three corner twist (ubr)+ (urf)+ (ufl)+: RU\'b2R'F\'b2L\'b2BL'(FL')\'b2B'L\line 
